# STRUGGLES.log

Open questions and confusions while working through the curriculum.
Format: numbered entry, module reference, the question, then `[OPEN]` /
`[ANSWERED]` status. Claude resolves `[OPEN]` entries during code review.

---

#01-classes:

#01-1 — What does `self` do? I thought it refers to the instance. [ANSWERED]

  Important correction: `self` does NOT refer to the instance.

  - `$this` → the instance. The thing you read/write properties on
    (`$this->title`).
  - `self::` → the **class itself**, captured at compile time (where the
    keyword was written). It's a *static* reference, used with `::` not
    `->`. You use it for class constants (`self::MAX`), static
    properties (`self::$count`), and calling sibling static methods.
  - `static::` → also a class reference, but resolves to the **runtime
    class** (the one that actually called the method, even if it's a
    subclass). This is "late static binding" — module 07 covers it in
    detail.

  Quick mental model: `$this` is the *object*, `self`/`static` is the
  *class*. They live in different worlds and use different operators.

  TS analogue: `this` ↔ `$this`. There's no clean TS equivalent of
  `self::`/`static::` — you'd have to write `(this.constructor as
  typeof Foo).bar`, which is ugly. PHP just gives you keywords.

#01-2 — Coming from functional JS, I barely used classes — public /
readonly / private feel new. [ANSWERED]

  Three quick rules of thumb:

  - `public readonly` → for **immutable** values you want callers to
    read directly. Value objects (Money, Email, BlogPost.title). It's
    the "frozen field" pattern.
  - `public` (no readonly) → only when you genuinely don't care if
    callers mutate it. Rare in good PHP code. Default away from this.
  - `private` → mutable internal state nobody outside should touch
    (counters, caches, the items array inside a Cart). Pair with
    public methods that mutate it safely.
  - `private readonly` → set once, never changes, only the class itself
    needs to read it (e.g. an internal timestamp). Great for things
    like `$createdAt`.
  - `protected` → only matters once subclasses come in (module 03). If
    you're not extending the class, prefer `private`.

  Mental shortcut: **default to `private`. Loosen only when you have a
  reason.** That's the opposite of how JS classes feel, where everything
  is public unless you explicitly add `#`.

#01-3 — `private readonly` + constructor body assignment: original
attempt errored, so I switched to declare-at-class-level + assign in
body. Why did the promoted version error? [ANSWERED]

  Your fix is correct and idiomatic — keep it.

  Why the original errored: when you write
  `private readonly int $createdAt` as a *promoted* parameter, PHP does
  the assignment for you the moment the constructor argument is bound
  to the property (before the body runs). At that point the readonly
  slot is already filled. Your `$this->createdAt = time();` in the body
  is a *second* write to a readonly property, which throws.

  Two valid shapes:

  ```php
  // (a) declare + assign in body — your current shape, good when the
  // value is computed (time(), uuid, etc.):
  private readonly int $createdAt;
  public function __construct(...) {
      $this->createdAt = time();
  }

  // (b) promoted param with caller-supplied value — when the value
  // comes from outside:
  public function __construct(
      private readonly int $createdAt,
  ) {}
  ```

  Rule: readonly = exactly one write. Promoted params count as that
  one write. If you also need the body to set it, *don't promote*.

  FOLLOW UP — why does `echo $BlogPost . PHP_EOL;` produce the same
  output as `echo $BlogPost->__toString(), PHP_EOL;` even though
  `__toString()` is never called explicitly? [ANSWERED]

  Because `__toString()` is a **magic method**. PHP calls it
  automatically whenever an object is used in a *string context* —
  string concatenation (`.`), double-quoted interpolation (`"...
  {$obj}"`), `(string) $obj` casts, `echo`, `print`, `sprintf("%s",
  $obj)`, and so on.

  In `echo $BlogPost . PHP_EOL;`:
  1. The `.` operator demands a string on each side.
  2. PHP sees `$BlogPost` is an object, looks up `__toString()`, calls
     it, gets back the string.
  3. Concatenates with `PHP_EOL`, hands the result to `echo`.

  So the explicit call and the implicit call go through the exact same
  method. Same input, same output.

  Magic methods you'll meet in PHP — they all start with `__`:
  `__construct`, `__toString`, `__get`/`__set` (property accessors,
  Laravel uses these heavily), `__call` (method intercept), `__invoke`
  (makes an object callable like a function), `__destruct`. They're
  "hooks" the language calls automatically at specific moments.

  JS analogue: `Symbol.toPrimitive` / `toString()` / `valueOf()`. When
  you do `` `${obj}` `` or `String(obj)` or `"x" + obj`, V8 looks up
  those hooks. PHP's magic methods work the same way — different names,
  same idea.

  Tiny stylistic note: `echo $a, $b;` (comma) and `echo $a . $b;`
  (concat) both work, but they differ slightly: comma passes two args
  to `echo` (no concat allocation), concat builds one string first.
  Output is identical; `echo` is one of the few language constructs
  that accepts a comma-separated list. Don't lose sleep over which to
  pick — `echo $obj . PHP_EOL;` is the most common.


#02-visibility:

#02-1 — `catch (\Error $e)` doesn't fire — the script crashes anyway. Why? [ANSWERED]

  PHP has **two separate throwable hierarchies**, both implementing
  `\Throwable`:

  ```
  \Throwable           (interface — everything below implements this)
   ├── \Error          (engine/runtime errors)
   │    ├── \TypeError
   │    ├── \ValueError
   │    └── (the readonly violation from module 01 — this one)
   └── \Exception      (application-level "exceptional" cases)
        ├── \LogicException
        │    └── \InvalidArgumentException   ← what assertPositive throws
        └── \RuntimeException
  ```

  Module 01 worked because writing to a readonly property throws
  `\Error`. Here, your `assertPositive` does `throw new
  \InvalidArgumentException(...)` — that's an `\Exception`, **not** an
  `\Error`. The two are siblings, not parent/child. So
  `catch (\Error $e)` doesn't match, the exception propagates up, the
  script dies with the "Uncaught" fatal you saw.

  Fixes:

  - `catch (\Exception $e)` — catches everything in the Exception tree
    (including InvalidArgumentException).
  - `catch (\InvalidArgumentException $e)` — narrowest, catches exactly
    what you threw. **Preferred in real code** — narrow catches make
    intent clear and avoid swallowing unrelated bugs.
  - `catch (\Throwable $e)` — catches both trees. Use sparingly,
    usually only at the very top of an app for last-ditch logging.

  Rule of thumb: `\Error` = "the engine is mad at me." `\Exception` =
  "my domain logic said no." Catch the narrowest type you can.

#02-2 — "Promote the parent's balance" option: does that mean changing
`private float $balance` to `protected float $balance` so the child can
read it as `$this->balance`? [ANSWERED]

  Yes, exactly. `protected` = "this class and any subclass can see it."
  Under that option, `SavingsAccount::applyInterest()` could do:

  ```php
  $this->balance *= (1 + $this->interestRate);
  ```

  …and it would work. The child reaches into the parent's state
  directly.

  Why option 2 (the helper) is the better default: with `protected`,
  the child has *unrestricted* access — it can set the balance to
  anything, including negative values, bypassing every invariant the
  parent set up. With a `protected function setBalance(...)`, the
  parent stays in charge of the rules; the child only requests a
  change. This is **encapsulation via methods, not properties** — it's
  the same instinct as React's "lift state up + pass setters down"
  pattern.

  Concretely: if next week you add "balance can never exceed
  $1,000,000" as a rule, option 1 forces you to add that check in
  every subclass that touches `$this->balance`. Option 2 forces you to
  add it in one place — the helper.

#02-3 — Is `setBalance(...)` in the parent + calling it from the child
the right way to do this? [ANSWERED]

  The *pattern* (protected mutator in parent, called from child) is
  correct and idiomatic. But the **implementation** of your setBalance
  has issues — see the chat review for specifics. The current code
  takes the rate and multiplies the balance by it, which destroys the
  balance instead of growing it.

  A cleaner shape: keep `setBalance` truly a setter (assigns a new
  value, validates it), and let `applyInterest` do the math:

  ```php
  // parent
  protected function setBalance(float $newBalance): void {
      if ($newBalance < 0) {
          throw new \InvalidArgumentException("Balance can't go negative.");
      }
      $this->balance = $newBalance;
  }

  // child
  public function applyInterest(): void {
      $this->setBalance($this->balance * (1 + $this->interestRate));
  }
  ```

  Now `setBalance` does one thing (set), `applyInterest` does one
  thing (compute next balance), and each is testable in isolation.
  Same SRP principle React hooks push you toward.
  
  FOLLOW UP — tried `$this->balance` in the child, got "undefined
  property balance" (warning). Only promoting balance to `protected`
  let the child read it. Why? [ANSWERED]

  Because `private` means **"this class only"** — it hides from
  subclasses too. When `SavingsAccount` writes `$this->balance`, PHP
  looks for a property accessible from `SavingsAccount`'s scope.
  There isn't one — the parent has it, but it's `private`, so the
  child can't see it. PHP silently treats it as undefined: emits a
  warning, returns `null`, keeps going. `null * 1.02 = 0`, so the
  balance got wiped each time.

  Two valid ways out:

  - **Promote `$balance` to `protected`** (option 1 from the README).
    Works because `protected` = "this class + subclasses." But now any
    future subclass can stomp the balance directly, bypassing every
    invariant.
  - **Call the public getter** (`$this->balance()`). The public method
    is accessible from anywhere — including the child. Read through
    the getter, write through the setter, parent stays in full
    control of every change. This is the option you ended up with,
    and it's the right call.

  The asymmetry worth remembering: PHP is **strict about writes**
  (writing to a `readonly` or undefined-typed property → fatal
  `\Error`), **lenient about reads** (reading an undefined property →
  just a warning + `null`). Same engine, different policies depending
  on direction.


#03-inheritance:

#03-1 — What does `static::class` do? I just called it inside a
variable and returned it. [ANSWERED]

  `static::class` is the **runtime class name** — the class that was
  actually instantiated, even when the method is defined in a parent.

  In your code, `describe()` lives only in `Notification`. But when
  you call it on an `UrgentNotification` instance, `static::class`
  resolves to `"UrgentNotification"` — *the subclass*, not
  `"Notification"`. You can see this in your output:

  ```
  <Notification>:           ← from $notification
  <UrgentNotifiaction>:     ← from $urgentNotification (typo aside)
  <SmsNotification>:        ← from $smsNotification
  <FinalEmailNotification>: ← from $finalEmailNotification
  ```

  Four different strings, one shared method. That's the payoff.

  Contrast with `self::class`:
  - `self::class` = always the class where the keyword is *written*
    (here: `"Notification"`). Resolved at compile time.
  - `static::class` = the class that actually called the method.
    Resolved at runtime. This is "late static binding" (module 07).

  Why it matters: this is how Laravel's `Model::find($id)` returns a
  `User` when you call `User::find(1)` and a `Post` when you call
  `Post::find(1)`. The `find()` method lives in the base `Model`
  class, but `static::` (and `new static()`) make it know which
  subclass you actually invoked. You're meeting the feature now;
  module 07 explores the factory-method angle.

  TS analogue: there isn't a clean one. The ugly version is
  `(this.constructor as typeof Foo).name`. PHP just gives you a
  keyword.

#03-2 — Tried `public function __construct(private string $sender) {
parent::__construct($sender); }`, but then I couldn't figure out how
to handle the `$message` param. Adding `parent::__construct($sender,
$this->message)` errored. So I added a `protected string $message` in
the child too. Right approach? [ANSWERED — but the current code has
the wrong shape, see chat review]

  Wrong instinct, common one. Two corrections layered together:

  1. **Why `$this->message` errored.** When the parent constructor
     hasn't run yet, `$this->message` is uninitialized — reading a
     typed uninitialized property throws an `\Error`. The parent
     constructor is what *assigns* `$this->message`, so you can't
     read it *before* calling parent.

  2. **Why redeclaring `$message` in the child isn't the fix.** The
     parent already has `protected string $message`. Adding
     `protected string $message` to the child constructor (via
     promotion) creates a second property with the same name — PHP
     allows it, but it's redundant and confusing. The parent's slot
     is the one that should hold the message.

  The real shape: the child's constructor should accept *all the
  parent's args* (`$recipient`, `$message`) **plus** the new ones the
  child needs (`$sender`). Pass the parent's share to
  `parent::__construct`, keep the rest as the child's own:

  ```php
  class SmsNotification extends Notification
  {
      public function __construct(
          string $recipient,
          string $message,
          private string $sender,
      ) {
          parent::__construct($recipient, $message);
      }
  }
  ```

  Notice `$recipient` and `$message` have **no visibility keyword** —
  they're plain parameters, consumed and forwarded, *not* promoted to
  child properties. The parent owns them. The child only promotes
  `$sender` because that's the new state it's responsible for.

  General rule for overriding constructors: **child accepts a
  superset of parent's params, forwards parent's share, promotes only
  the new ones.**

#03-3 — Attempted `class x extends FinalEmailNotification {}` and got
"Cannot extend final class" fatal at parse time. [ANSWERED]

  Exactly the behavior you wanted to confirm. `final class` means
  "the inheritance chain stops here." The fatal happens at *parse
  time* (when PHP loads the file), not at runtime — there's no way
  to wrap it in `try/catch` from inside the same file, because the
  file fails to even load. That's why you correctly left the
  forbidden extension commented out in `run.php`.

  Worth knowing for module 09 (Capstone) and real Laravel work:
  `final` on a class is the modern PHP default for value objects and
  infrastructure. The Symfony / Laravel ecosystem leans heavily on
  it now — it makes refactors safer because no one can silently
  build on a class you don't intend to be extensible.


#04-abstract:

#04-1 — Used `sprintf` and `%s` from the materials but not sure how
they work. [ANSWERED]

  `sprintf` = "string printf." Same idea as `printf` but it **returns**
  the formatted string instead of echoing it. Templating without
  concatenation.

  - `%s` is a placeholder for "insert a string here." `sprintf` walks
    the format string left to right, replacing each `%s` with the next
    argument (after the format string itself).
  - Other common placeholders: `%d` (integer), `%f` (float), `%.2f`
    (float with 2 decimals — actually handy for money), `%05d`
    (int padded to 5 digits with zeros).

  Your call:

  ```php
  sprintf(
      'Charged %s via %s',
      number_format($this->amountCents / 100, 2),   // → first %s
      static::class,                                 // → second %s
  )
  ```

  is equivalent to:

  ```php
  'Charged ' . number_format(...) . ' via ' . static::class
  ```

  Both produce the same string. `sprintf` reads better when you have
  more than two pieces to glue together, or when the format pattern is
  worth seeing at a glance (e.g. money formatting, log lines). For
  two-piece concat with `.`, `.` is fine. Pick whichever reads clearer
  in the moment.

  JS analogue: there's no built-in `sprintf` in vanilla JS. Template
  literals (`` `Charged ${x} via ${y}` ``) replaced the need. PHP added
  string interpolation too (`"Charged {$x} via {$y}"`), so you'll see
  both styles in real codebases.

#04-2 — Every subclass that extends the abstract class warns "does
not implement method 'charge'" if I leave `charge()` out. So abstract
methods are required for every subclass — right? [ANSWERED]

  Right. That's the whole point of `abstract`. The keyword on a method
  is a compile-time contract: **any non-abstract class that descends
  from this one MUST implement this method.** No exceptions, no
  defaults.

  - If you forget to implement it, the IDE warns first, then PHP
    fatals at *parse time* the moment it loads the file. Same level
    of strictness as the `final class` rule from module 03 — the
    engine refuses to load broken inheritance.
  - If you want to allow a subclass to skip implementing it, you have
    to make the subclass itself `abstract` (turtles all the way down).
    Eventually some concrete subclass has to do the work.

  Why this is useful: it lets the parent rely on the method existing
  without knowing how it works. Your `PaymentMethod::receipt()`
  doesn't call `charge()`, but if it did — `return $this->charge() .
  ' ' . static::class;` — it could trust that *every* concrete
  subclass has implemented `charge()`. The parent gets to call
  forward into code that doesn't exist yet, because the contract
  guarantees it will exist by the time the parent is actually used.

  This is the "template method" pattern. Laravel's `Notification`
  class works this way: it has abstract `via()` (which channels?) and
  abstract `toMail()`, and the base class orchestrates them.

#04-3 — `processAll` bonus: I don't understand "type-hint the
parameter against the abstract class," and I'm not sure how to iterate
a list of `PaymentMethod` since I can't instantiate the abstract
class. [ANSWERED]

  Two separate things tangled together. Let's untangle:

  1. **You can't instantiate `PaymentMethod`** — true, that's what
     abstract means. But you *can* instantiate `CreditCardPayment`
     and `PayPalPayment`, which are concrete subclasses. They **are**
     `PaymentMethod`s (because they extend it). So a list of them is
     still "a list of PaymentMethod objects" from the type system's
     perspective.

  2. **"Type-hint against the abstract class"** — PHP type-checks at
     the *type* level, not the *concrete class* level. A parameter
     typed `PaymentMethod` accepts any subclass:

     ```php
     function chargeOne(PaymentMethod $payment): void {
         echo $payment->charge(), PHP_EOL;
     }
     chargeOne($ccPayment);   // works — CreditCardPayment IS-A PaymentMethod
     chargeOne($ppPayment);   // works — PayPalPayment IS-A PaymentMethod
     ```

  For "list of payments," PHP has two idiomatic shapes:

  **Variadic** — clean, type-checked per element:

  ```php
  function processAll(PaymentMethod ...$payments): void {
      foreach ($payments as $payment) {
          echo $payment->charge(), PHP_EOL;
      }
  }
  processAll($ccPayment, $ppPayment);
  ```

  `PaymentMethod ...$payments` means "zero or more PaymentMethod
  arguments, collected into an array called `$payments`." Each call
  site is type-checked individually.

  **Array with docblock** — what you'd see in older code:

  ```php
  /** @param PaymentMethod[] $payments */
  function processAll(array $payments): void {
      foreach ($payments as $payment) {
          echo $payment->charge(), PHP_EOL;
      }
  }
  processAll([$ccPayment, $ppPayment]);
  ```

  The `array` type-hint here is *not* element-typed at runtime — PHP
  doesn't have generics. The docblock is the only thing telling tools
  (PHPStan, IDEs) what's inside. Pick variadic when you can; it's
  enforced.

  TS analogue: variadic = `(...payments: PaymentMethod[]) => void`.
  Same shape.
